description_block:
The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaLes Fables de La Fontaine: a joined figurative panel of toile, together with further toiles
French, late 18th century and later
The first, circa 1800, with figurative vignettes against an architectural ground, illustrating scenes from La Fontaine's Fables, including 'Le Rat et l'Elephant' with a caparisoned elephant and 'Les deux Taureaux et la Grenouille', backed and lightly quilted, together with:
a fragmentary panel of figurative toile de Jouy, circa 1790s, with figures fishing, riding and dining with landscape motifs;
a quilted pelmet of toile printed with garlanded vignettes of the Three Graces, second half 18th century, with Dimitrios, New York label;
another quilted panel of toile, circa 1830s, with Dimitrios, New York label;
and two further examples of quilted toile, and a pelmet of block printed cotton
the first approx.: 126 x 200 cm (7)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA varied selection of velvet, to include two panels of ciselÃ© silk velvet
French, early 20th century and later
The ciselÃ© silk velvet examples woven with a 'bubble' spot motif design, the first with red spots on a blue silk taffeta ground, the second with blue spots on a purple ground, with Dimitrios, New York label, together with:
a panel of olive green cut and uncut velvet, woven with a zig-zag motif, with French label and later Marvic Textiles label inscribed 'Zigzag 90588/to be returned after development'; 
a fragment of Art Deco cut and uncut velvet, with Marvic Textiles label inscribed 'Art Deco Velvet/French';
a section of burgundy cut and uncut velvet, with Dimitrios, New York label inscribed 'Lyon, France/1840-1860', possibly later;
a length of green striated cut velvet;
and an L-shaped piece of printed velvet
the larger 'bubble' example: 58.5cm x 43cm (7)

The olive green example was the source inspiration for the 'Zigzag 90588' Marvic Textiles design.

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA selection of cut and ciselÃ© velvet fragments and panels
Comprising:
a length of cut velvet, woven in green, pink and yellow against a buff ground, Italian 20th century, with Bevilaqua Venezia and stamped 'Soprano Jardinere vert strie', rolled;
a panel of green and yellow cut velvet, woven with a lattice of daisy motifs, one selvedge, with Dimitrios, New York label;
another panel of cut velvet, woven with acanthus leaves in pink and green against a buff ground, probably French, early 20th century, with old label for 'N.B.S & Co.';
two fragments of Baroque style ciselÃ© velvet, woven in pink and green with an acanthus scroll design against an ivory ground, rolled;
a corner of burgundy cut velvet, with one green selvedge;
and a panel of green cut velvet, woven with chrysanthemums against a yellow ground, stapled to an associated golden yellow damask of the same design, 20th century, with old label in French and later Dimitrios, New York label
the first approx.: 69cm x 63cm (8)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA selection of three resist block-printed and dyed indigo cotton panels, together with two Empire-style printed cotton panels, and a panel of fine cotton
French, early 19th century
Comprising: a resist block-printed panel with a lattice of flower and leaf motifs, with a striated finish, probably circa 1800;
a fragment with a rosette and foliate resist-dyed pattern, one selvedge, probably 19th century;
a fragmentary resist-dyed quilted panel, with flowerhead medallions within a floral lattice, circa 1800;
an Empire-style panel with resist block-printed rosette medallions against an indigo ground;
another example discharge printed with columns of roses between bands of vermicular patterning on an indigo ground, French or English, 19th century;
and a fine cotton example with leaf-encircled flower medallions printed in madder against a saffron yellow ground, previously quilted, with label inscribed 'Veg Dye C1810'
the largest Empire-style panel: 80cm x 50cm (6)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA panel of block printed and resist dyed cotton, together with further printed cotton examples
The first possibly Indonesian, or Indian for the Indonesian market
The first with bell-shaped motifs infilled with boteh, against a resist-dyed indigo ground, with some damages, together with:
a fine 19th century Persian printed square cotton cover, block printed and painted with a red flower-sprigged field and lattice borders in red and blue, with a Dimitrios, New York label;
a fringed floor spread of printed and painted cotton with a lattice field, early 20th century;
an early 19th century Indian panel of saffron block printed cotton;
and another panel of printed cotton, affixed with Dimitrios, New York label, possibly French, 1930s
the floor spread approx.: 240cm x 162cm (5)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA cot quilt of printed cotton, together with two further printed cottons
Probably Indian, 19th century
The first block printed with a lattice of blue rosettes and pinecones against a rust red ground, with cotton backing, with some damages;
together with a French 18th century double-sided quilted panel of printed cotton, with wide lacey ribbons, roses and carnations against a rust red ground, damages;
and a single curtain of printed cotton toile, composed of two lengths printed in deep red with classical figures, columns, cherubs and trophies, with two fringed edges, the top edge with a wide pencil heading 
the curtain: 323cm x 149cm (3)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA narrative toile depicting Le Premier Navigateur
French, late 18th century
Printed in black with illustrative vignettes narrating the story of Melide from the 1785 French pantomime ballet Le Premier Navigateur, with Dimitrios, New York label inscribed 'FRANCE/late 18th c.';
together with a length of toile printed in tan with scenes from the life of Joseph, with Dimitrios, New York label 'French, 18th c.';
a panel of block printed linen with wide bands of madder red, mounted to card (damages), with Dimitrios, New York label 'NANTES, Fr./EARLY 18thc./De Jouy';
and a large panel of red ground toile composed of three joined lengths, late 19th century or later, with Emma Hurley label
the first: 94cm x 80cm (4)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA selection of three French 18th century floral chintz panels, together with two further panels
The first chintz valance, possibly late 18th century, with a large-scale floral meander, three edges trimmed with binding, joined;
the second printed with a meander of exotic flowers, in red, purple and pale blue;
the third a length of block printed chintz, possibly linen, with stripes of boteh shaped flowers against a spotted ground, applied with Marvic Textiles label inscribed 'INDIAN MOTIF STRIPE VERSION', with damages and repairs;
the fourth printed chintz, possibly Indian, 18th/19th century, with sprays of carnations within boteh motifs against a delicate floral ground;
and a cover of printed linen, block printed with offset rows of flowerheads, with a later ruffled braid trim, with Dimitrios label inscribed 'Oberkampf/18thC/France/ De Jouy'
the largest: 190cm x 65cm (5)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA selection of French printed panels
Late 18th / 19th century
To include:
a panel of printed chintz, 19th century, with palm trees and flowers between scalloped band of deep blue and red, with Dimitrios label inscribed 'French/Provincial/c.1820-30', damages and repairs;
a panel of printed linen, with baskets of flowers between lacey ribbons, applied with Dimitrios label and adhesive Marvic Textiles label 'FLOWERS & LACE', probably French, 19th century;
a panel of printed cotton, attributed to Alsace, late 18th century, block printed with large scale hibiscus flowers and swags of drapery, backed with linen and quilted with hand stitching, some damages;
a panel of printed cotton, 19th century, with figurative roundels between stylised flowers, in red, blue and green, with Dimitrios label inscribed 'France, 1880s';
and a border fragment of block printed linen, printed in ochre with stylised leaf and flower motifs, possibly Indian
the largest example, with hibiscus flowers: 97cm x 75cm (5)

Various panels in this selection served as source material for later Marvic Textiles designs. The panel with printed palm trees and flowers was the source of inspiration for the 'Coromandel 7261' pattern, the panel with hibiscus flowers inspired 'Hibiscus 6220', and the border fragment inspired 'Beaulieu 8020'.

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA selection of eight printed cotton and linen panels and toiles
Early 19th century and later
To include: 
an early 19th century joined panel of narrative cotton toile, roller-printed in burgundy with a series of vignettes of Mary Queen of Scots and courtiers, applied with 'Jacaranda Archives Limited' label;
a Chinoiserie panel with figures playing musical instruments against a duck-egg blue ground, applied with label 'MARVIC TEXTILES / JACARANDA / ORIGINAL DOCUMENT', and with further Jacaranda Archives label;
a 20th century French toile panel in the Empire style, with figurative roundels of courting couples, applied with 'Design Source International New York', label;
a panel of Chinoiserie toile with figures beneath arbours, in the late 18th century style, applied with Dimitrios label and adhesive 'MARVIC TEXTILES/PAGODA TOILE', together with four others
the first: 143cm x 104cm (8)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA large selection of floral printed linen and chintz
19th century and later
To include:
a panel of floral printed linen, the flowers against a black ground, the lower edge fringed;
a length of printed Chinoiserie chintz, with pagodas and palm trees against a yellow ground, very fragile, possibly 18th century, relating to Marvic's Mandalay (Les Palmiers Collection);
a panel of yellow striped fabric with lacey ribbons, with Dimitrios, New York label, and Marvic Textiles sticker 'Posy Stripe', possibly early 20th century;
a panel of printed linen, with large scale peony bouquets against a polka dot ground, with Dimitrios, New York label 'FRc1880' and later Marvic Textiles sticker 'Polka Dot Roses;
a panel of printed crewel-work style chintz, with a stylised leaf and flower meander imitating embroidery, 20th century, two selvedges with one stamped '5285 1';
and a further selection of printed linen and chintz
the first approx.: 122cm x 144cm (11)

The Chinoiserie chintz with pagodas and palm trees is the source inspiration for the 'Mandalay' design from the Marvic Textiles Les Palmiers collection.

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaThree panels of toile de Jouy, together with some toile swatches, and two panels of chintz
French, early 19th century and later
The first panel entitled The Roman, circa 1820 after the 1811 design by Jean-Baptiste Huet for Oberkampf Manufactory, of deep red and yellow cotton toile de Jouy, printed with figurative scenes, including the story of Romulus and Remus, applied with Dimitrios, New York label, together with: another 20th century example of almost identical design in a yellow-ground toile;
another example, printed in red with classical figures and trophies, with fringing to the upper and lower edges;
the sample book of various swatches of toile in different colour ways, from Paule Marrot;
and two panels of floral chintz, one with Travers, New York label, stamped 'Carnavalet lime green'
The Roman panel: 82cm x 54cm (6)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaTwo fragmentary panels of printed linen, together with a panel of checked printed cotton and another of printed chintz
French, 18th century and later
The first printed with a large-scale floral meander, French or English, with Marvic Textiles label inscribed 'Scrolly Stripe';
the second printed with garlanded bouquets of peonies, backed with cotton support, with Dimitrios label inscribed 'De Jouy/Fr C1730s';
the cotton example with floral sprigged stripes, with Dimitrios label inscribed 'FR.18thc.';
and the final 19th century chintz example with delicate rose and carnation sprays, with Dimitrios label inscribed 'Alsace/1850s'
the largest: 80.5cm x 99cm (4)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA selection of silk damasks
20th century
Comprising: a length of striped red and gold silk damask, possibly early 20th century, with label for Jacaranda Archives; 
a group of four fragments of silk damask, woven in shades of red and pink, various sizes and some damage;
a length of olive green silk damask, with old shop label and later label for Ilene Danchig;
a fragment of golden silk-mix damask, with label for De Witte-Lietaer 'Chambord';
a panel of pale pink and green silk-mix figured silk, 20th century, with label for Industrie Tessili Alois, Italy;
and a fragment of yellow damask
the first approx.: 142cm x 130cm (9)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA selection of silk fragments and panels, to include a panel of French 19th century green silk moirÃ© damask
The damask with a Neoclassical design of urns of flowers and floral swags, with Docuswatch, New York label, together with:
a panel of red silk brocatelle, woven with urns of large-scale flowers, with old label inscribed 'Brocatell 65/rouge/St.Simon', French, early 20th century;
a panel of green silk damask woven with large-scale acanthus design, with Dimitrios, New York label;
a panel of ice blue silk damask, woven with flowering urns flanked by peacocks and swags and flowers, probably French, 19th century,
a three further panels;
and a selection of yellow silk damask fragments, reputedly from the walls of the Dining Room from Spencer House
the first approx.: 76cm x 67cm (qty)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA selection of textile fragments
To include: 
part of a shaped pelmet of striped satin with broad bands of green and crimson between fine golden stripes, double-sided, with scalloped lower edge and applied Dimitrios, New York label;
four small fragments of indigo resist-dyed cotton dress fabric, mounted to card;
and a group of six fragments of checked and chevroned linen, probably French, 18th and 19th century, including one indigo plaid example affixed with Design Source International, New York
the pelmet: 66cm x 25cm (11)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA chasuble of crimson velvet, with embroidered orphrey bands
The orphrey bands Spanish or Italian, 16th century
The embroidered bands worked in silks and metal threads with intricate foliate scrolls, possibly a later velvet, with later blue linen lining, some damages, together with:
a fragment of green and gold silk damask, woven with Neoclassical design of flaming urns, cherubs and scrolls, early 19th century;
a fragment of gold silk brocade, woven with pink fruit and exotic blue flowers against a golden ground, possibly Spitalfields, 18th century later mounted to card stamped Docuswatch Inc. to reverse;
a panel of striped silk and cotton, with Docuswatch, New York label;
and a further silk and velvet brocade panel, possibly for an altar
the chasuble approx.: 109cm x 69cm (5)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA selection of velvet panels, brocatelle and velour
Early 20th century and later
Comprising: a pieced panel of figured cut velvet with yellow and orange green garlands of flowers against a buff ground, probably early 20th century;
a fragmentary panel of blue ciselÃ© velvet;
a small panel of figured velour, with Dimitrios, New York label;
a panel of green cut velvet, with machine stitched edges, probably 20th century;
a panel of blue and gold figured velvet, woven with a Baroque style leafy lattice, probably Italian, 20th century;
and a seat panel of rust coloured brocatelle figured with urns, pine cones and pomegranates, probably Italian
the first approx.: 75cm x 129cm at widest point (6)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA selection of velvet panels, fragments and samples
19th/20th century
To include:
a panel of green ciselÃ© velvet, woven with an acanthus leaf and pomegranate design in a velour pile, 20th century;
two small samples of black cut velvet, each mounted to Dimitrios, New York card, one inscribed 'Leon 1845-1850', the other 'Lyon, France 1867';
a panel of striped yellow and grey velours, with Dimitrios, New York label;
a panel of striated orange cut velvet, with Dimitrios, New York label;
six smaller fragments of various velvet;
and a panel of brocade, woven with fleur de lys motifs, in red and metal thread, trimmed with a braid edging, with Emma Hurley label
the first: 72.5cm x 63cm (12)

The Personal Archive of Peter AfiaA selection of ikat weave panels and fragments
To include: 
An Indonesian cloth of fringed cotton ikat, probably circa 1900, affixed with Dimitrios, New York label;
a length of silk and cotton satin weave ikat, woven with green chevrons, with label for Emma Hurley Home Embellishments and Antiques, probably 19th century;
three various panels of striped flammÃ© ikat weave fabric;
and a group of six small fragments of striped cotton, including two swatches affixed to card from The Design Source International, New York
the Indonesian example approx.: 176cm x 70cm (qty)
